Chapter: BootPaint
  cites Foreword chapter CCE (to-unicode)
  grounds Device.Port

 The compiler's report channel on a board with no serial port and no proven
 ConOut. The screen is the only channel demonstrated to reach a human on the
 ASUS, which is why this paints; the trace is for the bed and comes after the
 paint on every rung, so a rung that dies inside its own print has already left
 its colour standing.

 The contract is the ladder's: the LAST COLOUR STANDING names the last stage
 passed. A stage paints on success and the payload spins on failure, so a dead
 machine still reports. The operator table lives in docs/HardwareSitting.md.

 Works chapter MetalLadder says all of this for a payload in the apps quire and
 cannot be cited from here: the compiler is the `codex` quire and apps are two
 quires to its right. It needs no foreword code either -- peek-qword, peek-32
 and poke-32 are builtins, so the whole channel is arithmetic.

 We say:

Section: Handoff

 The block cdx-to-pe.ps1's UEFI stub writes before it jumps, and its layout is
 that stub's contract rather than ours. The magic is the eight ASCII bytes
 "CDXHANDF" read as one little-endian quadword; the framebuffer base is the
 quadword at +16; width and height share the one at +24 and stride the one at
 +32, because the stub stores those four as 32-bit fields.

 The stub writes the header unconditionally and jumps over the body when
 LocateProtocol(GOP) returned nothing, so a zero base means "no framebuffer",
 which is also the answer when there is no block at all. That is what makes
 every rung below free on a run that is not a flight.

  bp-cell : Integer = 126976

  bp-magic : Integer = 5063257923364865091

 `hosted-kind` is asked FIRST and it is not an optimisation. The cell is a
 fixed PHYSICAL address only a bare-metal payload can reach, so on a hosted
 target this peek faults rather than answering, and it faults before the
 compiler has run a single rung. Every other path in this chapter is behind
 this one, because bp-base, bp-armed, bp-paint and the clock all reach here
 first.

  bp-present : Integer -> Boolean
  bp-present (dummy) =
   if hosted-kind /= 0 then False
   else peek-qword bp-cell 0 == bp-magic

  bp-base : Integer -> Integer
  bp-base (dummy) = if bp-present 0 then peek-qword bp-cell 16 else 0

  bp-w : Integer -> Integer
  bp-w (dummy) = bit-and (peek-qword bp-cell 24) 4294967295

  bp-h : Integer -> Integer
  bp-h (dummy) = bit-shru (peek-qword bp-cell 24) 32

  bp-stride : Integer -> Integer
  bp-stride (dummy) = bit-and (peek-qword bp-cell 32) 4294967295

Section: Palette

 Framebuffer pixels are B,G,R,X in memory order and poke-32 stores
 little-endian, so a 0xRRGGBB literal lands correctly.

  bp-cyan : Integer = 65535

  bp-yellow : Integer = 16776960

  bp-magenta : Integer = 16711935

  bp-orange : Integer = 16744448

  bp-blue : Integer = 255

  bp-white : Integer = 16777215

 Green is absent because the stub holds dark green while it runs, so a green
 screen already means the payload never reached its first rung. Red is the
 first rung's predecessor and nothing ever paints it, so a red screen would
 mean the palette itself is being read wrong.

  bp-red : Integer = 16711680

Section: Channel

  bp-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-cols (base) (stride) (y) (w) (colour) =
    if w <= 0 then 0
    else let p = poke-32 base ((y * stride + w - 1) * 4) colour
    in bp-cols base stride y (w - 1) colour

  bp-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-rows (base) (stride) (w) (h) (colour) =
    if h <= 0 then 0
    else let r = bp-cols base stride (h - 1) w colour
    in bp-rows base stride w (h - 1) colour

  bp-band : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-band (base) (stride) (w) (y) (yend) (colour) =
    if y >= yend then 0
    else let r = bp-cols base stride y w colour
    in bp-band base stride w (y + 1) yend colour

 A phase that runs for minutes with no rung of its own leaves one colour
 standing, and a stalled payload and a working one are then the same picture.
 The bar is that phase's only report on a board with no serial port, so it is
 drawn before the work it measures rather than after it.

  bp-progress-height : Integer
  bp-progress-height = 12

  bp-progress : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-progress (done) (total) (y) (colour) =
    let b = bp-base 0
    in if b == 0 then 0
    else if total <= 0 then 0
    else bp-band b (bp-stride 0) ((bp-w 0) * done / total) y (y + bp-progress-height) colour

 The readback answers whether the store landed at the base the stub published.
 It does NOT answer whether that base is the panel.

  bp-paint : Integer -> Integer
  bp-paint (colour) =
    let b = bp-base 0
    in if b == 0 then 0
    else let f = bp-rows b (bp-stride 0) (bp-w 0) (bp-h 0) colour
    in if peek-32 b 0 == colour then 1 else 0

 bp-hold repaints rather than spinning on an empty loop. A pure
 non-terminating function is not a halt: the optimizer removed the first
 version of the ladder's outright, so a FAILED stage fell through and painted
 the colour that means the stage passed. The framebuffer writes are what make
 this one survive.

  bp-hold : Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold (colour) =
    let f = bp-paint colour
    in bp-hold colour

 Every hold is guarded, and that is the difference between this and the
 ladder's. A probe runs only on the board, so an unguarded spin has still
 reported. The compiler runs everywhere, and an unguarded hold turns a
 host-side compile error into a hang.

  bp-hold-if-armed : Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold-if-armed (colour) =
    if bp-base 0 == 0 then 0 else bp-hold colour

 A full-screen hold repaints over the text ConOut just wrote, and on a board
 whose only log is the glass that destroys the report the operator was meant to
 read. This holds in a band along the bottom instead, so a printed diagnosis
 survives the colour that follows it.

  bp-hold-band : Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold-band (colour) =
    let b = bp-base 0
    in let f = bp-band b (bp-stride 0) (bp-w 0) ((bp-h 0) - bp-progress-height) (bp-h 0) colour
    in bp-hold-band colour

  bp-hold-band-if-armed : Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold-band-if-armed (colour) =
    if bp-base 0 == 0 then 0 else bp-hold-band colour

  bp-hold-when : Boolean, Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold-when (yes) (colour) = if yes then bp-hold-if-armed colour else 0

  bp-truth : Boolean -> Integer
  bp-truth (b) = if b then 1 else 0

  bp-armed : Integer -> Boolean
  bp-armed (dummy) = bp-base 0 /= 0

  bp-pass : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-pass (ok) (colour) (prev) =
    if ok == 0 then bp-paint prev else bp-paint colour

  bp-hold-if : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-hold-if (ok) (prev) = if ok == 0 then bp-hold-if-armed prev else 0

Section: Clock

 The CMOS RTC through ports 0x70/0x71, which is the only wall clock a
 UEFI-tenant payload has without installing a timer. External format:
 register 0 is seconds, 2 minutes, 4 hours, 7 day of month, 8 month, 9 year;
 register 10 bit 7 is update-in-progress and a read taken while it is set can
 tear; register 11 bit 2 set means binary values, clear means BCD. Reads are
 armed-guarded so nothing outside a flight touches the ports.

  bp-cmos : Integer -> Integer
  bp-cmos (reg) =
   let s = port-out-byte 112 reg
   in port-in-byte 113

  bp-bcd : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-bcd (mode) (v) =
   if bit-and mode 4 == 4 then v
   else (v / 16) * 10 + int-mod v 16

  bp-rtc-wait : Integer -> Integer
  bp-rtc-wait (fuel) =
   if fuel <= 0 then 0
   else if bit-and (bp-cmos 10) 128 == 0 then 0
   else bp-rtc-wait (fuel - 1)

  bp-rtc-seconds : Integer -> Integer
  bp-rtc-seconds (dummy) =
   if bp-armed 0 == False then 0
   else let w = bp-rtc-wait 100000
   in let mode = bp-cmos 11
   in bp-bcd mode (bp-cmos 4) * 3600 + bp-bcd mode (bp-cmos 2) * 60 + bp-bcd mode (bp-cmos 0)

  bp-2d : Integer -> Text
  bp-2d (n) = if n < 10 then "0" & integer-to-text n else integer-to-text n

  bp-rtc-date-text : Integer -> Text
  bp-rtc-date-text (dummy) =
   if bp-armed 0 == False then "0000-00-00"
   else let w = bp-rtc-wait 100000
   in let mode = bp-cmos 11
   in "20" & bp-2d (bp-bcd mode (bp-cmos 9)) & "-" & bp-2d (bp-bcd mode (bp-cmos 8)) & "-" & bp-2d (bp-bcd mode (bp-cmos 7))

  bp-hms-text : Integer -> Text
  bp-hms-text (s) = bp-2d (s / 3600) & ":" & bp-2d (int-mod (s / 60) 60) & ":" & bp-2d (int-mod s 60)

Section: Phase Log

 The compile's own build log, banked while the pipeline runs. The pipeline is
 pure and its metrics records die before the footer, so phase-measure pokes
 one row per phase into the spare half of the handoff page -- the stub owns
 126976..131071 and writes 48 bytes of it -- and the DISK footer walks the
 rows after the verdict. A row is three qwords: the label's first eight
 characters packed one byte each, the wall seconds the phase took, and the
 megabytes its bivy grew. The strip is the live half: one segment painted per
 phase at y 24..35, under the two progress bars, so an ORANGE hold shows
 which phase it is sitting in.

  bp-log-count-cell : Integer = 128000

  bp-log-start-cell : Integer = 128008

  bp-log-rows : Integer = 128016

  bp-log-row-max : Integer = 24

  bp-poke64 : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-poke64 (addr) (v) =
   let lo = poke-32 addr 0 (int-mod v 4294967296)
   in poke-32 addr 4 (v / 4294967296)

  bp-pack8 : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-pack8 (t) (i) (n) (acc) =
   if i >= 8 | i >= n then acc
   else bp-pack8 t (i + 1) n (acc + bit-shl (int-mod (to-unicode (char-code (char-at t i))) 256) (i * 8))

  bp-strip-colour : Integer -> Integer
  bp-strip-colour (i) =
   when int-mod i 6
    is 0 -> bp-cyan
    is 1 -> bp-yellow
    is 2 -> bp-white
    is 3 -> bp-orange
    is 4 -> bp-blue
    is otherwise -> bp-red

  bp-seg-cols : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-seg-cols (base) (stride) (y) (x0) (n) (colour) =
   if n <= 0 then 0
   else let p = poke-32 base ((y * stride + x0 + n - 1) * 4) colour
   in bp-seg-cols base stride y x0 (n - 1) colour

  bp-seg-rows : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-seg-rows (base) (stride) (x0) (n) (y) (yend) (colour) =
   if y >= yend then 0
   else let r = bp-seg-cols base stride y x0 n colour
   in bp-seg-rows base stride x0 n (y + 1) yend colour

  bp-strip : Integer -> Integer
  bp-strip (i) =
   let b = bp-base 0
   in if b == 0 then 0
   else let seg = (bp-w 0) / bp-log-row-max
   in bp-seg-rows b (bp-stride 0) (int-mod i bp-log-row-max * seg) seg 24 36 (bp-strip-colour i)

  bp-log-phase : Text, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bp-log-phase (label) (took) (mb) =
   if bp-armed 0 == False then 0
   else let i = peek-qword bp-log-count-cell 0
   in if i >= bp-log-row-max then 0
   else let row = bp-log-rows + i * 24
   in let p0 = bp-poke64 row (bp-pack8 label 0 (text-length label) 0)
   in let p1 = bp-poke64 (row + 8) took
   in let p2 = bp-poke64 (row + 16) mb
   in let c = bp-poke64 bp-log-count-cell (i + 1)
   in bp-strip i

Section: Trace

 The trace is what the BED reads, and it is not the flight's channel: a run
 with no handoff block says nothing at all, so a normal compile's output is
 byte-for-byte what it was. It comes after the paint on every rung, which is
 the ladder's whole contract -- a rung that dies inside its own print has
 already left its colour standing.

 Every trace line is built INSIDE its guard. Codex is strict, so a
 `disk-say (... & ...)` would concatenate on every call and leave the string in
 an arena nothing reclaims -- on a run that then prints nothing, and on
 `opening`'s rung that means every compile the fleet runs.

  disk-mark : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-mark (tag) (ok) (colour) (prev) = act
   fb <- bp-pass ok colour prev
   disk-trace tag ok fb
  end

  disk-trace : Text, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-trace (tag) (ok) (fb) =
    if bp-armed 0
    then print-line-uni ("DISK-LADDER " & tag & " ok=" & show ok & " painted fb=" & show fb & " base=" & show (bp-base 0))
    else print-text ""

 disk-stop is the last thing a stage does, never the first. A hold that ran
 before the stage's own diagnostic swallowed it: CODEGEN-HALTED and
 DISK-OUT: FAILED both stopped being printed on an armed run, which took the
 two states they name off the trace the bed reads.

  disk-stop : Boolean, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-stop (yes) (colour) = act
   h <- bp-hold-when yes colour
   disk-stopped colour
  end

  disk-stopped : Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-stopped (colour) =
    if bp-armed 0 then print-line-uni ("DISK-LADDER stopped at " & show colour)
    else print-text ""

  disk-rung : Text, Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-rung (tag) (ok) (colour) (prev) = act
   disk-mark tag ok colour prev
   h <- bp-hold-if ok prev
   disk-continuing tag
  end

  disk-continuing : Text -> [Console] Nothing
  disk-continuing (tag) =
    if bp-armed 0 then print-line-uni ("DISK-LADDER " & tag & " continuing")
    else print-text ""
